cast:
  Zofia:
  Grace:
  Ollie:
  Morwenna:
  Kit:
  Ruth:
  Mara:
  Hugh:
  Darwin:
  Ada:
  Hale:
  Niamh:
  Banda:
~~~~
at-the-grave
---
Zofia: Alone. They've put ‘alone’ on your caption.
Darwin: I had not supposed the place of burial would become a place of employment. What is the paper you are holding? [class=dead]
Zofia: The Witness Workshop's caption. Last month's edition. “Darwin alone first presented natural selection to the public.”
Darwin: Alone? Wallace's essay was there too. I had supposed that part would survive as plainly as a label on a specimen. What does your record say? [class=dead]
Zofia: I've got the reading record. Alfred Russel Wallace's essay in 1858 prompted the joint reading of your papers at the Linnean Society on 1 July 1858. Lyell and Hooker arranged it. Their caption leaves Wallace out.
Darwin: Then I should ask them to restore his part in that presentation. The reading record seems a better starting point than my expectation. [class=dead]
Zofia: Come with me. We have a meeting across the road. You can help check the sources.
Darwin: If I may accompany you, yes. Though I should also be interested in observing when these voices become audible to you— [class=dead]
Zofia: No experiments on me. People already watch my face when I stop talking.
Darwin: Then I shall confine the inquiry to the papers. Your refusal is quite sufficient. [class=dead]
Grace: Are we going? I've been studying your coat for ages. It has not evolved pockets large enough for that folder.
Zofia: He'll come. He's agreed about Wallace. Look, this source list calls his voyage an expedition under Captain Cook.
Grace: That's the rejected list. The checked one says HMS Beagle, Captain Robert FitzRoy, naturalist Darwin. It began on 27 December 1831. Nearly five years. You turned over two sheets together.
Zofia: Oh. Fine. This one says The Voyage of the Beagle, 1839; On the Origin of Species, 1859; The Descent of Man, and Selection in Relation to Sex, 1871. Those match the catalogue.
Darwin: A barnacle collected on a voyage may occupy one for years afterwards. I should keep those dates apart. Has this catalogue been checked? [class=dead]
Zofia: He's asking whether we checked the catalogue.
Grace: Tell him yes. And tell him the appointment does not move to a later century because we're late.

three-parts
---
Kit: First the Commons, then—sorry. Start with all three. House of Commons. House of Lords. The Crown. That's Parliament. You need that before you ask where the Bill goes.
Zofia: And which room do we get?
Hugh: A meeting room, for today. Neither House is sitting in this room, and our signatures here cannot give you an Act.
Kit: Commons and Lords consider legislation. Both Houses must agree the text; Royal Assent from the Sovereign makes it an Act. At State Opening the monarch speaks in the Lords. Black Rod comes to summon the Commons. The Commons go to the Lords. Not the other way round. I've stopped getting that backwards.
Grace: Has your chair accepted its summons yet?
Kit: It was standing in for a member. It wasn't supposed to answer.
Ollie: Right, so your most reliable rehearsal partner is furniture. Quite a strong recommendation for furniture, actually.
Grace: But why do we still do this? When the camera shows everyone walking, does anybody thank the person who got the chairs out?
Kit: They thank the people at the front. I still need the chairs in the right places.
Grace: Then put whoever moved them on your thank-you list.
Kit: I'll ask whose name to put. Before they go home.

opposite-benches
---
Mara: Zofia, sit by me. I've put my name on the sponsorship sheet. Hugh, yours goes beside it.
Hugh: The clause first, Mara. My signature afterwards, if we agree.
Ollie: Labour and Conservative, in the same meeting. Should I put the biscuits in the middle?
Hugh: Within reach would be an adequate constitutional arrangement.
Mara: Labour grew from the labour movement; it began in 1900 as the Labour Representation Committee. You can put me beside a Conservative without changing that.
Hugh: The Conservative Party took its nineteenth-century form in 1834, associated with Peel's Tamworth Manifesto. “Tory” is the older name you will also hear. Neither history decides this clause for us.
Grace: You face each other in there. Here you want the same biscuit. Which bit is the disagreement?
Mara: Labour has the government benches; I'm a government backbencher. Hugh sits opposite, with the Conservative opposition. A party doesn't own a side for ever. Some of my colleagues think asking a minister nicely is enough. I want a duty to answer a supported application, with reasons.
Hugh: Some of mine would preserve the record by leaving it untouched. I want the original retained, with a correction attached when the evidence warrants one. An unexamined cupboard is a poor definition of preservation.
Zofia: So you'll both sign.
Hugh: We have not reached the sentence which prevents me signing.
Mara: Hugh, point to it. We have until the escort comes back.

belief-clause
---
Hugh: “Where the representative's account is believed by the sponsoring member, the requested correction shall be entered.” I do not believe her, and the Bill must not need me to.
Zofia: You could say that without looking at me.
Hugh: I could. The objection would remain. Belief in a witness and evidence for a particular claim are different things.
Ollie: An MP believes in ghosts? That would travel quite a long way down the corridor.
Mara: Ollie, keep it in this room. Zofia, I put that clause in. I wanted to stop them treating you as a nuisance. I made my belief do the work of a search. I'm withdrawing it.
Ada: If a member's conviction supplies the missing evidence, the operation returns whatever that member wishes. A machine so instructed would be obedient and useless. [class=dead]
Zofia: Ada says the clause just gives us whichever answer the member wants. But they can search for ever. Couldn't they replace the bad page now and keep the evidence somewhere else?
Hugh: Consider the Monument. Its anti-Catholic accusation was finally removed in 1830. That alteration is itself part of the record. If we kept only a clean account and destroyed the evidence of the accusation, how would the next reader discover who had been blamed?
Hale: If the instrument bears false witness against me, madam, let it be cancelled; saving the parchment profits the wrongdoer. [class=dead]
Zofia: Hale wants his false summary cancelled. He says keeping it helps whoever wrote it.
Grace: And what helps the next person who opens it? Your annotation needs to be on the page they actually see. My dad can't spend every evening explaining what somebody's file forgot.
Hugh: Yes. Retaining an error without a visible correction can keep doing harm. I would preserve both together, with the evidence and the responsible author's name.
Zofia: Together. Not “correction available on request”. All right. The replacement bit can go.
Hale: If my stated reason stands beside the charge, I shall hear your proposal; I do not thereby assent to the charge, nor surrender my objection. [class=dead]
Zofia: He'll listen. He's still cross.
Mara: Zofia, so am I. We can put a deadline and review into the proposal. We cannot put the answer in before the search.

one-source
---
Hugh: This account of the maintenance rescue has two supporting sources. My father's recollection, published in the staff bulletin, and the later anniversary booklet. Both credit the official alone.
Mara: Hugh, turn the booklet over. Who supplied its account?
Hugh: The staff bulletin. I had understood the editor to have interviewed—
Mara: The credit says “adapted from”. Even the wrong initial is the same.
Zofia: The comma. Both put it after “having secured the door”. That's a horrible place for a comma.
Darwin: The same misplaced comma in each vessel. I suspect we have transferred one specimen and counted it twice. [class=dead]
Zofia: Darwin thinks we've counted the same specimen twice.
Hugh: Your punctuation is not proof of copying. The printed credit is. Yes. I have counted a dependent account as corroboration.
Grace: Does that mean Vale's mother did the rescue?
Hugh: It means these pages cannot independently exclude her. I would need the maintenance evidence before saying what she did. Still less could I call her the sole rescuer.
Mara: His father's complaint asked for a search. They sent a tribute to everyone involved. That's in the reply he gave us. A tribute isn't a search.
Hugh: My father told me this account when I was a boy. I liked that he knew the man who had saved everybody. I have repeated it with less care than I required from you.
Ada: If an erroneous term is copied through a calculation, the number of pages does not diminish it. Nor would a garland placed upon the author correct the term. [class=dead]
Zofia: Ada says a garland won't correct it. There's a programming language called Ada now. Named after you. And Ada Lovelace Day, the second Tuesday of October. Those are on the checked page, Ada.
Ada: Then the honour may be considerable; yet it does not distinguish my Notes from the article I translated. Have they answered that distinction? [class=dead]
Zofia: No. They haven't answered. I thought you'd like to know about the day.
Ada: I am obliged to you for telling me. If praise could perform subtraction, however, your publisher would have no error left to amend. [class=dead]
Mara: Zofia, what did she say?
Zofia: That praise doesn't fix the caption. Menabrea wrote the article; she translated it and added her own Notes. We still need the editor's answer.
Grace: Why not just email the publisher?
Zofia: We did. Twice. We got receipts. Nobody's even told us who is checking.
Mara: Grace, I can put their silence in a public request and press for a response. That doesn't give me control of their press. It does mean someone has to decide whether they're willing to keep ignoring a named question in public.
Hugh: And my support must not depend on their giving the answer we prefer. A searched, reasoned refusal has to remain possible.
Zofia: Even after all this?
Hugh: Especially then. Otherwise our search is another way of hiding an instruction to agree.
Niamh: And Mum is still waiting in Belfast. Can we give her envelope some of this appointment?
Zofia: Let us get the signatures first. We might not get them together again.

report
---
Morwenna: If you look at the headings first—please, before the times. These both say week two. Tuesday. Rehearsal. Samuel's entry is 14:10. The copied sheet says 14:40. I checked the date twice.
Zofia: The copy's wrong, then. We can ask Kit.
Morwenna: I already reported it. To Ms Achebe-Lane. I sent the pages and the names of everyone who was there.
Grace: You promised you wouldn't give her our names.
Morwenna: I promised. Then I told her. I thought if something happened and I had kept it—
Grace: Something has happened. You told her my dad was part of it before you told us you were going to.
Morwenna: I said what his entry showed. I didn't accuse him.
Grace: Did you send the bit where you promised us?
Morwenna: No.
Darwin: I think both pages must be kept. The disagreement is a reason to examine their descent, not to discard the less convenient specimen. [class=dead]
Zofia: Darwin says keep both. I want copies of both, Morwenna. With the headings. Don't crop anything to make it tidier.
Morwenna: I can give you what I kept. If she hasn't told me— No. I kept them. You can have them.
Ruth: Morwenna, copies into separate sleeves. Zofia, future visits require a fresh written decision. Today's escort will take you out when this meeting ends.
Zofia: We haven't finished asking about the response sitting.
Ruth: That request goes in writing.
Grace: You knew she'd do that.
Morwenna: I knew she might.

later-sitting
---
Banda: Ollie, your place is the gallery seat named on the booking. You wait for the escort afterwards. Being my son does not make the door yours to open.
Ollie: I know. I told someone I knew where everything happened, which was optimistic. I mostly know where Dad tells me to stop.
Mara: Zofia, I'll present the paper petition at this sitting. The request for debate time is separate. So is the Bill. I have agreed to sponsor it; I have not made it law.
Zofia: The title on the packet. It's my name under “representative”. Not Ada's.
Mara: Yes. And you have left room for an answer you may hate.
Zofia: Don't sound pleased about that bit.
Banda: Zofia, the waiting place is here. Ms Achebe-Lane's decision has arrived for you.
